To focus on an element found by `getElementsByTagName` in JavaScript, you can use the `focus()` method. However, to do this, you need to get a reference to the element first.

Here's an example:
```
// Get all elements with the tag name "input"
var inputs = document.getElementsByTagName("input");

// Use getElementsByTagName to get the first input element
var firstInput = inputs[0];

// Focus on the first input element
firstInput.focus();
```
This will focus on the first input element found on the page.

If you want to focus on a specific element, you can use a more specific selector, such as `document.querySelector` or `document.getElementById`:
```
// Focus on the first element with the class "myInput"
document.querySelector(".myInput").focus();

// Focus on an element with the id "myInputElement"
document.getElementById("myInputElement").focus();
```
Keep in mind that not all elements can be focused, and the `focus()` method may not work as expected for certain types of elements.

Also, note that if you're trying to focus on an element that's not currently part of the DOM (e.g., it's being loaded dynamically), you may need to use a technique like `setTimeout` or `MutationObserver` to wait for the element to be added to the DOM before trying to focus on it.

